{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And of every living thing.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this to include the demons, and the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded him to bring them into the ark. And it may be explained plausibly that it was impossible for them to remain in their place, which is the air within the spheres of the elements, because the decree was to destroy all flesh in which is the breath of life, and this includes all possessors of the locomotive soul upon the earth, and He further added ״from under the heaven״ — for the decree of the Flood was also upon the creatures that are beneath the spheres of the elements, and these are the demons; therefore it was necessary to bring them into the ark lest they be obliterated in the air that is beneath the heaven." "<b>And what</b> He said, ״you shall bring into the ark,״ is not to be understood as though he would literally bring them, for it is written immediately after, ״they shall come to you to keep alive,״ — for of their own accord they would come; rather, the intent when He said ״you shall bring into the ark״ is that he should assist them in their entry and exert himself on their behalf that they might live. Now this was by way of command, but in actual deed — where do we find that the demons came into the ark among the rest of the species? It is where it is written: ״וכל העוף למינהו כל צפור כל כנף״. ״וכל העוף למינהו״ — these are unclean birds. ״כל צפור״ — these are clean birds. ״כל כנף״ — these are the demons, for they fly in the air. And the verse supports this immediately: ״ויבאו אל נח אל התיבה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:54.654001169Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}